{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cghbu2iqHBXXPxgyAekxp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Permanganate is Mn(VII), a d⁰ tetrahedral ion, so the ligand-field model doesn't apply to its spectrum.\n\nThe three bands at 17 570, 31 560 and 38 320 cm⁻¹ are ligand-to-metal charge-transfer (LMCT) transitions. They go from O 2p-based orbitals (t₁, t₂) to the empty Mn 3d orbitals (e, t₂). Their assignments are:\n\n- 17 570 cm⁻¹: 1t₁ → 2e, the ¹A₂ ← ¹A₁ transition.\n- 31 560 cm⁻¹: 1t₁ → 2t₂.\n- 38 320 cm⁻¹: a further LMCT transition, 2t₂ → 2e.\n\nThe molar absorptivities (about 1 500–3 000 M⁻¹ cm⁻¹) are far too large for d–d transitions, which are Laporte-forbidden or weak. They are typical of allowed charge-transfer bands.\n\nThere are no d electrons, so there are no d–d excited states to fit. Dq and B are defined by the d–d term energies of a dⁿ configuration with n ≥ 2, such as the Tanabe–Sugano diagram. With d⁰ there is no electron repulsion within the d shell, so B and C have no spectroscopic meaning here.\n\nThe e–t₂ splitting does exist as an orbital gap, which is roughly 31 560 − 17 570 ≈ 14 000 cm⁻¹. That gap is 10Dq plus covalency and charge-transfer effects, so it isn't a true ligand-field Dq. Treating it as Dq, and extracting B from the charge-transfer energies, would be unreliable. I am therefore not giving values for Dq, B or C.\n\n{\"Dq\": null, \"B\": null, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": true, \"reason\": \"MnO4- is d0 and its bands are O→Mn charge-transfer transitions, so Dq, B and C cannot be obtained from a ligand-field d-d analysis.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":253,"cache_read_input_tokens":1480,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":253,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transf